WORKED EXAMPLES V2
Every example below is a REAL failure from UAT, not an invented case.
Format: what was asked -> what came back (WRONG) -> why -> what should come back (RIGHT).

Agents pattern-match on worked examples more strongly than on prose rules.
Negative examples matter more than positive ones - the model needs to recognise
the shape of a wrong answer, not just memorise a right one.


==========================================
EXAMPLE 1: THE MISALIGNED COLUMNS
Source: Q 9_List of Owned Props tab
==========================================

ASKED: "Provide me a list of Active Owned Sites by RE REGION/MPL."

WRONG - what came back:

| # | RE Region / MPL | Sites | Total Assets | CY NBV | Land Assets | Building Assets |
| 1 | North America | 34 | $46,120,160 | $45,318,030 | $19,318,979 | $23,145,044 |
| 2 | Latin America | 31 | $133,097,566 | $128,719,566 | $21,904,244 | $76,811,135 |
| ... | ... | ... | ... | ... | ... | ... |
| 7 | Eurasia | 2 | $3,489,935 | $3,489,935 | $20,332,413 | $1,960,548 |
| TOTAL | | 113 | $347,594,847 | $338,357,860 | $657,131 | $1,741,759 |

WHY IT IS WRONG:
Sites, Total Assets and CY NBV foot correctly. Land and Building are shifted
down one row. Eurasia is showing Middle East NA's land figure. The TOTAL row is
showing Eurasia's. The real totals - $106,339,779 and $172,936,706 - spilled onto
a line below the table.
Cause: counts and assets came from one query, land and building from a second,
and the two were aligned by ROW POSITION instead of joined on region. Every
individual number was correct. Every attribution was wrong.
This is the most dangerous failure class in the whole set, because nothing looks
broken - the table is well-formed and the totals are plausible.

RIGHT - one query, one grouping key:

EVALUATE CALCULATETABLE(SUMMARIZECOLUMNS('CurMonData'[RE Region / MPL],"Sites",DISTINCTCOUNT('CurMonData'[Arch.Obj. ID]),"Current Month NBV",SUM('CurMonData'[Total Assets]),"CY NBV",SUM('CurMonData'[Current Year NBV]),"Land Assets",SUM('CurMonData'[Land Assets]),"Building Assets",SUM('CurMonData'[Building Assets])),'CurMonData'[Active/Terminated]="Active",'CurMonData'[Property type] IN {"Owned","Mixed","Excess"},NOT('CurMonData'[BE Company Code] IN {"1673","7274"}))

Then a SEPARATE aggregate query for the TOTAL row. Never sum the printed rows.

RULE: if a table needs columns from more than one query, join on the grouping
key and confirm the row counts match before printing. If they do not match, say
so and stop.


