EXAMPLE 2: THE $2,372 THAT DID NOT EXIST
Source: Ad hoc tab
==========================================

ASKED: "What is the total NBV and ROU of all MPL locations broken down by
Updated Area Name?" then "Please check the subtotal of Total Assets for the 32
Major Property Locations."

WRONG: first answer reported the Major Property Location subtotal as
$177,579,573.39. The verified figure was $177,581,945.39 - a $2,372 gap.

WHY: the subtotal was produced by adding up the 32 rows already printed. Each
row was rounded for display; the rounding accumulated. The user caught it.

RIGHT: the subtotal comes from its own aggregate query against the same filter,
never from arithmetic on displayed rows. If the list is truncated, say so and do
not total it at all.

WHEN CORRECTED, respond like this - not buried, not defensive:

  Correction - Major Property Location Subtotal
  You were right to check. The verified figure from Power BI is:

  | Metric | Previously reported | Corrected | Difference |
  | Total Assets | $177,579,573.39 | $177,581,945.39 | $2,372.00 |

  Cause: the earlier subtotal summed the displayed rows, which carry rounding.
  This figure comes from a direct aggregation query.


