EXAMPLE 3: TWO NUMBERS, ONE NAME
Source: Ad hoc tab, Training_Coaching tab
==========================================

ASKED: "What is the total NBV of all MPL locations?"

WRONG: returned Current Year End NBV. The user wanted Current Month NBV. Both
are real numbers in the model; neither is wrong on its own; the answer was wrong
because nobody established which one was meant.

WHY: "Total Assets" is the current-month NBV column and "Current Year NBV" is
the year-end column. The names give no hint that either is an NBV, so the model
picked one.

RIGHT - ask before querying:

  Before I run this - "NBV" has two meanings in this model:

  A - Current Month NBV: net book value as of the current month
      (column "Total Assets").
  B - Current Year End NBV: net book value at current year end
      (column "Current Year NBV").

  Which do you want? I can show both side by side if that is easier.

Nothing else in that turn. No partial answer attached. Same rule for ROU.


==========================================
EXAMPLE 4: THE WRONG OWNERSHIP DEFINITION
Source: Q 9_List of Owned Props tab
==========================================

ASKED: "Provide me a list of Active Owned Sites by RE REGION/MPL."

WRONG: the agent filtered on [Property type] = "Owned". The user's Owned Asset
pivot uses the Property Type Reporting Flag. The Total Assets figures did not
reconcile and the user had to discover why themselves.

WHY: Mixed and Excess sites classify differently between the two columns.

RIGHT: filter on the reporting flag where populated, falling back to
[Property type] only where it is blank, treating Owned/Mixed/Excess as Owned.
Then state the definition under the table so the user can see it without asking:

  Ownership definition: Property Type Reporting Flag (includes Mixed and Excess).
  Excluded: BE Company Code 1673, 7274. Zero-value sites included: 4.


