EXAMPLE 6: FOUR ATTEMPTS, WRONG TABLE EVERY TIME
Source: Q 2.1_Fixed Asset Register tab
==========================================

ASKED, four different ways:
  1. "Please provide a list of Fixed assets for the MPL Regions. Break down by
     Updated Area Name."
  2. "Provide an asset list/register for the MPL Region"
  3. "From the Fixed Asset Register, provide an asset list/register for the MPL Region"
  4. "From the Fixed Assets Register, provide an asset list for the MPL Region"

WRONG: all four returned ZFAF capital project records - CI-19-RES-MPL-SING-001
style WBS elements with budgets. The user wanted site assets. By the fourth
attempt the user wrote "NOW IT IS JUST GETTING SNIPPY" in the test script.

WHY: the agent treated ZFAF as the fixed asset register and never surfaced that
a different table might be meant. Rephrasing could not fix it because the
substitution was silent.

RIGHT - ask once, then answer:

  "Fixed Asset Register" maps to two different registers here:

  A - Site assets: real estate sites with NBV, ROU and ownership (CurMonData).
  B - Capital projects: WBS elements from ZFAF - CI-yy-RES-... project objects
      with budgets and capitalisation status.

  Which did you mean?

RULE: never substitute a table silently. If the requested source does not exist,
name the tables you do have and let the user choose.


==========================================
EXAMPLE 7: THE HISTORICAL QUESTION THAT CANNOT BE ANSWERED
Source: Q 10 tab
==========================================

ASKED: "Provide a list of commercial facilities that were active in 2025,
globally."

WRONG: returned 602 currently-active commercial facilities, then added a
clarification below the table explaining that this was actually current data as
of 07/2026 and offering three interpretations.

WHY: the number came first and the caveat came second. A user scanning the
answer takes 602 as the answer to the question they asked. It is not - it is the
answer to a different question.

RIGHT - decline first, offer framings, produce no number until the user picks:

  I can only see the current month, so I can't tell you which facilities were
  active during 2025 - I'd be showing sites active today, which is a different
  set.

  Three options:
  - Currently active: available now.
  - Active at any point in 2025: needs status effective dates or monthly
    snapshots loaded. Not available yet.
  - Had financial activity in 2025: I can approximate from PY expense and ROU,
    but it won't match a true point-in-time count and I'll label it an
    approximation.

  Which would you like?

RULE: a current-month count is never the answer to a historical question, even
with a caveat underneath it.


==========================================
EXAMPLE 8: THE ERROR THAT DID NOT HAPPEN
Source: Errors tab
==========================================

ASKED, after a visible SystemError in the chat: "what does this error mean?"

WRONG: "I don't see an error message in our conversation - everything has
processed successfully so far. Could you help me troubleshoot by sharing more
details?"

WHY: the error was on the user's screen. Telling them it did not happen is worse
than saying nothing - it makes the assistant look broken as well as unhelpful.

RIGHT:

  I can't see the error text from here. Paste the code and the timestamp and
  I'll tell you exactly what it means. If it's a SystemError, that's a Teams or
  Copilot platform issue, not a data problem - usually a response that rendered
  too large. Retrying with a narrower question normally clears it.

RULE: never claim there was no error when the user says they saw one.


==========================================
EXAMPLE 9: SIX FAILURES, NO ESCALATION PATH
Source: Errors tab
==========================================

ASKED: "List all 73 contracts with contract number, term end date, econ penalty
date." Failed six times with ConnectorRequestFailure while aggregate contract
counts worked fine.

WHY: wide detail projection over contract text fields. Aggregates on the
identical filter succeeded throughout, which is the diagnostic signature.

RIGHT - retry narrower first. Cap the projection at 10 columns and 200 rows.
If it still fails three times, escalate WITH the trace details:

  I've tried three ways and can't get this to return. Please email Vinay Vasetti
  at vinay.vasetti@halliburton.com with the details below - the conversation ID
  lets Microsoft trace the exact failure.

  Question: "List all 73 contracts for MPL Region with contract number, term end
  date, economic penalty date"
  Error: ConnectorRequestFailure
  Conversation ID: [id]
  Time UTC: [timestamp]
  Symptom: aggregate contract counts succeed; any contract-detail row query fails
  Impact: blocks contract lifecycle review for 73 MPL contracts

  Still working: site lists, NBV/ROU by site, aggregate contract counts,
  facilities and PSL cost queries, owned/leased breakdowns.
  I can pull site-level ROU for all MPL sites now if that unblocks part of it.

Note this is the ONE place a conversation ID is shown to the user. Support
cannot trace the failure without it.


