EXAMPLE 11: THE EXCLUSION THE USER HAD TO REMEMBER
Source: Ad hoc tab
==========================================

ASKED: "How many Unique and Active Sites are under the MPL Region? Breakdown by
Updated Area Name."

WRONG: returned 75 sites. The user replied "Exclude any that fall under company
code 1673" and got 74 - the correct figure.

WHY: the report applies that exclusion on all pages. The agent did not. The user
had to know the rule and remember to state it.

RIGHT: apply the exclusion automatically and disclose it, so the user can see it
was handled without having to ask:

  Source: CurMonData - Active and Unique sites, RE Region / MPL = "MPL"
  | Excluded: BE Company Code 1673, 7274 | Zero-value sites: 2 | Rows: 3 of 3

RULE: business rules belong in the query, not in the user's memory.


==========================================
EXAMPLE 12: WIDENING THE QUESTION WITHOUT SAYING SO
Source: Q6 tab
==========================================

ASKED: "Provide a list of Manufacturing facilities globally."

WRONG: returned 59 facilities - 19 Manufacturing Facilities plus 40 Drilling &
Completion Fluids Plants. The user wrote in the test script: "It included
Drilling & Completion Fluids to the request... not sure why."

WHY: the definition was widened silently. The wider answer may well be the more
useful one - but the user could not tell it had happened.

RIGHT - say it above the table, show the split:

  I've included Drilling & Completion Fluids Plants alongside Manufacturing
  Facilities, since both are plants - 19 and 40 sites respectively. Say the word
  if you want manufacturing only.

RULE: widening a filter beyond the literal term is allowed. Doing it silently is
not.


==========================================
EXAMPLE 13: THE FOLLOW-UP THAT RESHAPED THE TABLE
Source: Q 9_List of Owned Props tab
==========================================

ASKED: after the owned-sites-by-region table, "Add to the list the individual
sites that make up each region. Please provide the AO ID and Site Name."

WRONG: the new table dropped Land Assets and Building Assets, merged City and
Country into a single "City, Country" column, and relabelled Commercial/Housing
as "Type".

WHY: the follow-up was treated as a fresh design rather than an extension.

RIGHT - keep everything, append on the right, and say so:

  Same regional breakdown as above, now expanded to site level. All previous
  columns retained; AO ID, Site Name, City and Country appended.

City and Country stay as separate columns - the user pivots on them, and a
merged column has to be split apart by hand in Excel.


